    <h2>Common Mistakes When Converting ACDT to EST</h2>

    <article class="mistake-card">
      <h3>1. Forgetting the 30-Minute Fractional Offset</h3>
      <p>
        The most frequent mistake is assuming South Australia follows a whole-hour offset like Eastern Australia (AEST, UTC+10). Treating ACDT as UTC+10 results in a 30-minute calculation error on every appointment.
      </p>
    </article>

    <article class="mistake-card">
      <h3>2. Misinterpreting the Calendar Day Rollover</h3>
      <p>
        Because EST is 15.5 hours behind ACDT, morning meetings scheduled on Tuesday in Adelaide take place on Monday afternoon in North America. Failing to double-check the calendar date leads to missed call appointments.
      </p>
    </article>

    <article class="mistake-card">
      <h3>3. Confusing ACDT with ACST</h3>
      <p>
        During the Australian winter (April to October), South Australia reverts to ACST (UTC+9:30). Using ACDT formulas year-round causes a 1-hour error during the southern hemisphere winter months.
      </p>
    </article>

    <article class="mistake-card">
      <h3>4. Misaligning Opposing Daylight Saving Calendars</h3>
      <p>
        Australia enters DST in October while North America leaves DST in November. During transition weeks, the offset changes dynamically between 13.5, 14.5, and 15.5 hours. Always verify exact dates using IANA timezone standards.
      </p>
    </article>
  </section>

